      "id": 1,
      "text": "Liverpool was originally called Little Ireland and was founded in 1797 by Irish settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://lpl.org/research/liverpool-ny/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village’s name was changed to Liverpool after the English city, echoing its salt‑producing heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liverpool%2C_New_York",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1857 Gleason Mansion, now a museum, is listed on both state and national historic registers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://villageofliverpoolny.gov/village-history/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Onondaga Lake Park, established in 1931, now occupies much of the old Oswego Canal bed that once brought Irish canal workers to the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liverpool%2C_New_York",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The local library hosts a walking tour and video collection that chronicles Liverpool’s history from its founding to today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
